---
description: Apply when exploring a dataset, answering questions with data, building metrics, or evaluating experiments
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Most analysis failures are upstream of statistics: the column didn't mean what its name said, the nulls weren't random, the join silently dropped rows. Never compute on data you haven't interrogated.
- Sharpen the question to decision shape (what exactly, which population, which window, what decision changes) and write down the expected result before looking — insurance against deciding the question after seeing the answers.
- Interrogate first: provenance (the generating process bounds what data can say) · per-column meaning (units, timezone, what null means, mid-history semantic changes) · quality sweep (row counts, duplicate keys, impossible values, null rates per period) · row counts before and after every join — fan-out/drop-out is the most common silent corruption · missingness structure (random or correlated with the outcome?).
- Look before summarizing: plot distributions (skew, bimodality, mass at zero), eyeball rows from both tails, pick summaries fitting the shape, segment the headline by the obvious cuts — aggregates reverse across segments.
- Exploration ≠ confirmation: patterns found by exploring are hypotheses, confirmed only on held-out data, the next period, or an experiment. Confirmation mode: metric/population/threshold defined before running, run once, no re-slicing to significance. Observational causality → "associated" + named confounders.
- Report effect size with uncertainty in decision units (never bare "significant"; give n), the chain of custody (source, filters, definitions, n per stage), caveats in the same breath, and "what would change this conclusion". If the data shows the question was wrong, that's the headline.
